    "q-75983": {
      "dom_id": "step-q75983",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$ and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{4}{q}_{4}.$ \n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $B=\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
