    "q-111515": {
      "dom_id": "step-q111515",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "The matrix $V$ is given below. Let ${v}_{1}$, ${v}_{2}$,  ${v}_{3}$, and ${v}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $V.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(V).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${v}_{4}={x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nV=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we reduce the matrix $V$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 6 & 8 & 12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 6 & 18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${v}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{4}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 3=9.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
